   "July 23, 1859, in Bkerke.": 1,
   "Hermitage:": 1,
   "from 1875 he lived at the hermitage near Annaya. See": 1,
   "the hermitage": 1,
   "Died:": 1,
   "December 24, 1898. He was struck ill while celebrating Mass on December 16 and died eight days later (Vatican). See": 1,
   "how he died": 1,
   "Beatified:": 1,
   "December 5, 1965, by Pope Paul VI, at the close of the Second Vatican Council.": 1,
   "Canonized:": 1,
   "October 9, 1977, by Pope Paul VI. He was the first Lebanese saint recognized in modern times (Vatican).": 1,
   "Feast:": 1,
   "July 24 in the Roman calendar, and the third Sunday of July in the Maronite calendar. See": 1,
   "the feast day": 1,
   "His Life in Brief": 1,
   "Charbel was a farmer's son who lost his father at the age of three. He longed for the monastery and entered it at 23. He spent years at the Monastery of Saint Maron at Annaya, and from 1875 lived as a hermit in prayer, fasting and work. After his death, crowds came to his tomb. The full story, with the timeline, is on the": 1,
   "Why People Pray to Saint Charbel": 1,
   "Pilgrims ask his intercession for healing and for peace. At Annaya, the monastery keeps a register of reported favors. More than 29,680 have been recorded since his death, according to Father Louis Matar, the monk who assesses and records them, as reported by CNEWA in March 2026. This is a monastery record, distinct from the Church's formal miracle approvals. See": 1,
